WEBVTT

1.1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.639
Almost every solid sinks in its own liquid. Drop solid wax into melted wax, and it sinks.

1.2
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.952
So why does ice float on water?

2.1
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:11.938
Here is the surprise.

2.2
00:00:11.938 --> 00:00:20.725
Liquid water at four degrees Celsius is denser than ice, about one gram per cubic centimeter versus point nine two.

3.1
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.658
The reason lives inside the molecule.

3.2
00:00:23.658 --> 00:00:30.364
Each water molecule has one oxygen and two hydrogens, bent at about one hundred and four degrees.

4.1
00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:39.210
Oxygen pulls electrons toward itself, so it is slightly negative and the hydrogens slightly positive.

4.2
00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.133
Neighboring molecules attract through hydrogen bonds.

5.1
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:46.744
In liquid water, molecules tumble around.

5.2
00:00:46.744 --> 00:00:52.461
Bonds constantly break and reform, so molecules can crowd close together.

6.1
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:57.143
When water freezes, molecules lock into a hexagonal lattice.

6.2
00:00:57.143 --> 00:01:02.961
Each one bonds to four neighbors at fixed angles, which holds them farther apart.

7.1
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:06.708
The lattice is full of empty space.

7.2
00:01:06.708 --> 00:01:14.173
Packed this way, the same molecules take up about nine percent more volume, so ice is lighter per unit volume.

8.1
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:21.085
Lighter per volume means it floats, with about nine tenths of an iceberg below the surface.

8.2
00:01:21.085 --> 00:01:23.735
Notice the ice sits mostly submerged.

9.1
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:31.259
So ice floats because hydrogen bonds force water into an open lattice when it freezes.

9.2
00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:36.317
That floating layer insulates lakes, letting life survive winter below.
